<section class="hero"><div class="wrap">
<div><p class="kick">Socks, taken seriously</p>
<h1>Most socks die in the wash, not on your feet.</h1>
<p class="lead">Knitfit is about the three things that decide whether a pair lasts a season or three years: what it is actually made of, how it gets washed and dried, and whether it suits the shoe it lives in.</p>
<p class="row"><a class="btn" href="#tools">Open the three tools</a> <a class="btn gh" href="sock-fibre-guide.html">Fibre guide</a></p></div>
<div class="quad">
<img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1576851932779-e06c35438d74?auto=format&fit=crop&w=600&q=75" alt="Assorted colourful socks" width="600" height="600" fetchpriority="high">
<img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1702816033462-27cbba324dae?auto=format&fit=crop&w=600&q=75" alt="A basket filled with socks beside a cup of coffee" width="600" height="600" loading="lazy">
<img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1734523856991-47c9a99308dd?auto=format&fit=crop&w=600&q=75" alt="A line of socks hanging from a tree" width="600" height="600" loading="lazy">
<img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1532629804872-98086b058cae?auto=format&fit=crop&w=600&q=75" alt="White and brown yarn in a basket" width="600" height="600" loading="lazy">
</div></div></section>
<section><div class="wrap"><div class="sec-h"><p class="kick">At a glance</p><h2>Five fibres, honestly compared</h2>
<p>Every sock is a blend, and the blend is usually printed somewhere on the packet. This is what each part of it does.</p></div>
<div class="tblw"><table class="ftab">
<thead><tr><th>Fibre</th><th>Best at</th><th>Weak at</th><th>Wears</th><th>Wash at</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><span class="dot" style="background:#B33A5B"></span>Cotton</td><td>Softness, price</td><td>Holds damp, slow to dry</td><td>Moderately</td><td>30&deg;C</td></tr>
<tr><td><span class="dot" style="background:#237A74"></span>Merino wool</td><td>Warmth wet or dry, no smell</td><td>Thin knits wear through</td><td>Well</td><td>30&deg;C, air dry</td></tr>
<tr><td><span class="dot" style="background:#E0A528"></span>Nylon</td><td>Abrasion resistance</td><td>Breathability</td><td>Longest</td><td>30&deg;C</td></tr>
<tr><td><span class="dot" style="background:#8A6FB0"></span>Bamboo viscose</td><td>Smoothness</td><td>Durability, holds damp</td><td>Poorly</td><td>30&deg;C, air dry</td></tr>
<tr><td><span class="dot" style="background:#4A7FC1"></span>Polyester</td><td>Wicking, drying speed</td><td>Holds smell</td><td>Well</td><td>30&deg;C</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p style="margin-top:1.2em;color:#5E5B52;font-size:.95rem">The elastane in the cuff is the part that decides whether a sock stays up, and it is the part heat destroys. That is why the wash matters more than the fibre.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="tools" id="tools"><div class="wrap">
<div class="sec-h"><p class="kick">Free tools</p><h2>Three tools, no sign-up</h2>
<p>All three run in your browser. Nothing is stored and nothing is sent anywhere.</p></div>
<div class="tk" id="life"><header><h3>Lifespan Estimator</h3><span>fibre, wear and washing</span></header>
<div class="body"><p>How long a pair should last at your wear and wash habits, and which habit is costing you the most.</p>
<div class="frow">
<div class="fld"><label for="lfFib">Main fibre</label><select id="lfFib"><option value="cotton">Cotton</option><option value="merino">Merino wool</option><option value="nylon">Nylon blend</option><option value="bamboo">Bamboo viscose</option><option value="wool">Thick wool</option><option value="poly">Polyester sport</option></select></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="lfWear">Wears a week</label><input id="lfWear" type="number" min="1" max="14" value="2"></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="lfTemp">Wash temperature</label><select id="lfTemp"><option value="warm" selected>40&deg;C</option><option value="cold">30&deg;C or cooler</option><option value="hot">60&deg;C</option></select></div>
</div>
<div class="fld"><label for="lfDry">Drying</label><select id="lfDry"><option value="air" selected>Air dried</option><option value="low">Tumble, low heat</option><option value="hot">Tumble, high heat</option></select></div>
<div class="out" id="lfOut"></div></div></div>
<div class="tk" id="pair"><header><h3>Shoe Pairing Picker</h3><span>height, cushion and fibre</span></header>
<div class="body"><p>What height, how much cushioning and which fibre suit the shoe you are actually putting them in.</p>
<div class="frow">
<div class="fld"><label for="prShoe">Shoe</label><select id="prShoe"><option value="boot">Boot or hiking</option><option value="trainer">Trainer or running shoe</option><option value="dress">Dress shoe</option><option value="loafer">Loafer or slip-on</option><option value="sandal">Sandal or clog</option></select></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="prSeason">Season</label><select id="prSeason"><option value="shoulder" selected>Spring or autumn</option><option value="summer">Summer</option><option value="winter">Winter</option></select></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="prAct">Mostly doing</label><select id="prAct"><option value="walk" selected>Walking</option><option value="desk">Desk work</option><option value="sport">Sport</option><option value="work">On your feet at work</option></select></div>
</div>
<div class="out" id="prOut"></div></div></div>
<div class="tk" id="blister"><header><h3>Blister Risk Checker</h3><span>friction, moisture and time</span></header>
<div class="body"><p>Where the risk is coming from on a long day, and which single change removes most of it.</p>
<div class="frow">
<div class="fld"><label for="blAct">Activity</label><select id="blAct"><option value="walk">Walking</option><option value="run">Running</option><option value="hike">Hiking</option><option value="stand">On your feet all day</option><option value="desk">Mostly seated</option></select></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="blFit">Sock fit</label><select id="blFit"><option value="good" selected>Correct size</option><option value="loose">Loose</option><option value="tight">Tight</option></select></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="blHrs">Hours on your feet</label><input id="blHrs" type="number" min="1" max="20" value="6"></div>
</div>
<div class="fld"><label for="blFib">Sock fibre</label><select id="blFib"><option value="merino" selected>Merino wool</option><option value="poly">Polyester sport</option><option value="nylon">Nylon blend</option><option value="cotton">Cotton</option><option value="bamboo">Bamboo viscose</option></select></div>
<div class="out" id="blOut"></div></div></div>
</div></section>
<section><div class="wrap"><div class="sec-h"><p class="kick">Long reads</p><h2>Two guides worth twenty minutes</h2></div>
<div class="gtwo">
<article class="gb"><div class="cap"><span>Guide one</span><h3>The Sock Fibre Guide</h3></div>
<div class="in"><p>What every fibre on the packet actually does, why the percentages are listed in that order, what elastane is for, how knit density changes durability, and which blends are worth paying more for.</p>
<p><a class="btn gh" href="sock-fibre-guide.html">Read the guide</a></p></div></article>
<article class="gb"><div class="cap"><span>Guide two</span><h3>The Washing &amp; Care Guide</h3></div>
<div class="in"><p>The temperature that matters, why tumble drying costs you months, washing inside out, sorting by fibre rather than colour, darning a heel in ten minutes, and storing socks without stretching the cuffs.</p>
<p><a class="btn gh" href="sock-care-guide.html">Read the guide</a></p></div></article>
</div></div></section>
<section style="padding-top:0"><div class="wrap"><div class="sec-h"><p class="kick">Questions</p><h2>What people ask</h2></div>
<div class="qa"><div><h3>Why do my socks go baggy before they wear out?</h3><p>Because the elastane fails first, and heat is what kills it. Hot washes and hot tumble drying break it down far faster than wearing does, which is why a drawer of saggy socks usually means a washing habit rather than cheap socks.</p></div><div><h3>Is cotton really a bad sock fibre?</h3><p>It is fine for a seated day and poor for anything else. Cotton holds water against the skin and dries slowly, and wet skin blisters at a fraction of the friction dry skin needs. For walking, sport or long days, merino or a sport polyester is a different experience.</p></div><div><h3>Does merino wool shrink?</h3><p>Washed cool it is stable. Washed warm, or tumbled hot, it felts, and felting cannot be undone. Thirty degrees and air drying is all it asks, and it does not need washing as often as cotton because it resists smell.</p></div><div><h3>How many pairs should I own?</h3><p>At least five of whatever you wear most, and ideally more. Elastane needs time to recover between wears, so a rotation of eight pairs lasts far more than twice as long as a rotation of four.</p></div><div><h3>Why does one sock always wear through first?</h3><p>Usually gait rather than the sock. Most people push off harder on one side, and many have a measurable difference in foot length. If the same spot goes every time, the shoe on that foot is worth looking at too.</p></div><div><h3>Do no-show socks ever stay put?</h3><p>Only with a silicone heel grip, and only if the size is right. Without one they walk down into the shoe within a couple of hours, whatever the price.</p></div><div><h3>Should socks be washed inside out?</h3><p>Yes. The inside collects skin, oils and most of the smell, so turning them out puts the dirty surface against the water. It also keeps the outside looking newer for longer.</p></div></div></div></section>
<section class="nl"><div class="wrap"><p class="kick" style="color:#DCEDEB">Newsletter</p><h2>The Knitfit Note</h2>
<p>One short email a month: one habit that doubles the life of a drawer, one fibre worth trying, and whatever has changed in the guides. Unsubscribe any time.</p>
